          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+algebra"} co-Heyting algebra
            it is possible to define an equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            operation in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mereology"} mereology
            without consideration of the embedding of bodies into an ambient space.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            be an element of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+algebra"} co-Heyting algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            with subtraction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo \
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \backslash
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+negation"} co-Heyting negation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            %strong (co-Heyting) boundary
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            is defined as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial a :=a\wedge\sim a
          %h2#properties Properties
          %blockquote
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial (a\wedge b) = (\partial a \wedge b)\vee (a\wedge\partial b)\quad
                \. (
                %strong> Leibniz rule
                )
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi b
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial (a\vee b)\vee\partial (a\wedge b) =\partial a\vee\partial b\quad
                \.
            %li
              %p
                The boundaries
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=\partial a
                can be characterized as those
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial x = x
                (in particular,
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mo ∂
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial^2=\partial
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim x=1
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong thin parts
            %li
              %p
                Every part is the sum of its
                %em regular core
                and its boundary:
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mo ∼
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=\sim\sim a\vee\partial a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial a
                as the
                %em irregular
                part of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          %h2#remark Remark
          %p
            As the lattice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtoposes
            of a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            comes naturally with a co-Heyting structure it becomes (in principle) possible to define the boundary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial\mathcal{A}
            of a subtopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            in this lattice and then in turn the boundary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial T'
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifies
            which is an extension of the theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            classified by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            (
            %a{:href => "#Law91a"}> Lawvere 1991
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Cara09"} Caramello 2009
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            When a subtopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is complemented in the lattice of subtoposes, as occurs e.g. for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subtopos"} open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subtopos"} closed
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+closed+subtopos"} locally closed
            subtoposes, its boundary vanishes:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial\mathcal{A}=0
            since in a co-Heyting algebra the complement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg a
            necessarily coincides with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim a
            (cf. at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+negation"} co-Heyting negation
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+negation"} co-Heyting negation
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+algebra"} co-Heyting algebra
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mereology"} mereology
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Closed boundaries of subtoposes are defined in an exercise of
            %a{:href => "#SGA4"} SGA4
            (cf. at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subtopos"} open subtopos
            for some of the details). The concept for co-Heyting algebras seems to stem from Lawvere (
            %a{:href => "#Law76"}> 1976
            ,
            %a{:href => "#Law86"}> 1986
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Law91a"} 1991
            %a{:href => "#Zar27"} article of M. Zarycki
            already studies properties and axiomatic potential of the boundary operator in topology.
            %a{:href => "#RRZ04"} La Palme Reyes, Reyes&amp;Zolfaghari (2004)
            has an introductory exposition in the context of bi-Heyting algebras. For boundaries of geometric theories
            %a{:href => "#Cara09"} Caramello (2009)
            is essential reading although they don’t appear there explicitly. For mereological applications of the concept see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Law86"} Lawvere (1986)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SW97"} Stell&amp;Worboys (1997)
            %a{:href => "#Pagl09"} Pagliani (2009)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Mor13"} Mormann (2013)
          %ul
            %li#SGA4
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Artin"}> M. Artin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+Grothendieck"} A. Grothendieck
                = succeed "," do
